## Do the errors follow 1/sqrt(n)? Yes, within what four single estimates can show.

- Each error is within its expected spread: all |err|/sigma lie in 0.13-1.26 (under the model |err|/sigma is
  half-normal; 90 % of draws fall in 0.06-1.96). Sum of squares 2.70 on 4 dof, p = 0.61.
- Log-log OLS slope over the four points: -0.65 (SE 0.21). Under the exact 1/sqrt(n) law, a 4-point slope
  from single estimates falls in [-0.94, -0.06] 95 % of the time (simulated); -0.65 is well inside
  (two-sided p = 0.42). With slope fixed at -1/2 the fitted constant is 0.90 (expected mean 1.31; sigma constant 1.64).
- Four points cannot pin the exponent down (the t-interval is [-1.54, +0.24]); the 1e7 error is smaller than the
  1e8 error, an ordinary fluctuation (z = 0.13), not a departure from the rate.
- Robustness check (off-cluster, numpy, independent replicates, n = 1e2..1e8): the RMS error follows
  1.659 n^(-0.5017 +- 0.0019) (weighted fit, chi2 = 2.7 on 5 dof), against the theory 1.642 n^(-1/2);
  RMS/sigma = 0.98-1.01 for n <= 1e7 (0.84 at 1e8 with only 20 replicates, SE ~ 0.12). This measures the
  rate itself, which the four single draws can only be consistent with.

